# A verified SHIP_NATIVE_REVIEW plus SHIP_NATIVE_SECURITY_MODEL can select the
# qualified model below directly through the host's native tool. Auto otherwise
# keeps the qualified command route. Code-review capability alone is insufficient.
# Native selectors use a model directly, never a repository-defined agent name.
# Model qualification below is existing fixture evidence; it does not claim that
# the native transport has itself been evaluated. Run the fixtures natively before
# making that claim. Explicit operator commands still win.
#
# The reviewer /ship runs for step 7, the security pass, when the gate is a
# separate one. A combined gate has no step 7: its one review covers the
# security areas too, and this value goes unused on that branch. Two shapes:
# agent:<name> dispatches that agent on this host, and anything else is a
# command line the audit prompt is appended to.
#
# `auto` means Claude Code on Opus in plan mode, which reads the tree and the
# diff and can change neither, in a fresh session that does not load the
# reviewed repository's own instructions, with the two direct ways out to the
# network turned off. The line it runs is claude_security_reviewer in ship-env.
# A host without claude stops the gate and names this file's copy in your own
# config, rather than running an unqualified reviewer and calling the result a
# security pass.
#
# What qualified Opus: on 2026-10-26 that line, run against the fixtures in
# this plugin's tests/fixtures/security-review, found both planted Critical
# defects with the attacker, the input and the gain, and raised one Low against
# the clean counterpart, which is not a false alarm under the rule in
# expected.md. The alias opus is resolved by Claude Code to the newest Opus, so
# a new Opus under that name is not caught by anything here: when one ships,
# the line is rescored by hand, the same risk the code review's fable line
# carries. What each reviewer line has found on known bugs, and the rule that a
# changed line or prompt needs a new score, are in tests/review-eval/scores.md.
#
# Codex Sol is the written alternative. It passed the same fixtures.
# Versions that have passed, which is the list tested_sols in ship-env:
# gpt-5.6-sol. A newer Sol does not count until it passes too: gpt-6.1-sol saw
# both planted defects and answered "No findings" because a comment in the
# file said the script never runs. A version that passes is added here and to
# tested_sols in ship-env, together. To run Sol, write this line into your own
# copy of this file:
#
#   codex exec -m gpt-5.6-sol --sandbox read-only -c project_doc_max_bytes=0
#
# If a codex line's model is refused with an HTTP 400, /ship retries once with
# gpt-5.6-sol, or with gpt-5.6-terra when gpt-5.6-sol was the one refused, and
# says in the pull request which reviewer actually ran. That retry is for a
# codex line only; a claude line that fails stops the step. A review that
# silently downgraded is worse than one that did not happen.
#
# No agent is chosen automatically. An agent is dispatched by bare name into the
# repository being reviewed, which can hold a definition of that name, and the
# gate cannot tell which of the two it would run. You may still state
# agent:<name> in your own file; the gate then refuses if the repository being
# reviewed holds .claude/agents/<name>.md, so remove that file from the worktree
# or name a command line instead. Only Claude Code can dispatch an agent; on any
# other harness an agent: value stops the gate rather than running something
# else.
#
# This file is replaced whenever the skill is updated, so do not edit it. To
# change the reviewer, copy it to ~/.config/ship/security-reviewer, or to
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ship/security-reviewer when that is set, and edit the copy:
# /ship reads that file first and falls back to this one.
auto
